e` \u2014 include 'empty': '' in video dict in test 3; assert no download call for it.\n\nAlso cover line 88-91 mobj present and absent \u2014 test 2 covers both (one with pattern, one without). Test 3's mp4 also has pattern.\n\nEdge: in test 2, video_urls from regex `({.+?})` on trans_options_str \u2014 non-greedy `.+?` followed by `\\s*,`. trans: `{\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/a_720p_1000.mp4\"}, \"sub\": \"https://example.com/v.srt\", ...}`. The regex `\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,` \u2014 matches from `{` after \"video\": minimal up to `}` followed by `\\s*,`. The first `}` is at end of `{\"mp4\": \"...mp4\"}` followed by `,` \u2014 good, matches `{\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/a_720p_1000.mp4\"}`. _parse_json parses it. \n\nWait \u2014 but the URL contains `//` \u2014 js_to_json: does it treat `//` as comment? The COMMENT_RE matches `//[^\\n]*` \u2014 but the alternation order: string literals are matched first in the regex alternation (`\"(?:...)*\"` comes before `{comment}`). The URL is inside double quotes in trans? No \u2014 options_str has single quotes: `'https://example.com/a_720p_1000.mp4'`. js_to_json regex: `'(?:[^'\\\\]*(?:\\\\\\\\|\\\\['\"nurtbfx/\\n]))*[^'\\\\]*'` \u2014 single-quoted string matched first, so `//` inside is protected. Good. But in test 2, the parse must FAIL. With `extra: 'a' + 'b'` \u2014 js_to_json: `'a'` \u2192 `\"a\"`, `+` stays, `'b'` \u2192 `\"b\"` \u2192 `\"a\" + \"b\"` invalid JSON. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the `//` in URLs within options_str for the title/poster regexes \u2014 those regexes run on options_str (raw) and trans_options_str. Fine.\n\nAlso in test 2, the video regex runs on trans_options_str where URLs are double-quoted \u2014 fine.\n\nOne more check for test 2: `_html_search_regex` calls `_search_regex` which... uses `self._downloader`? _search_regex may report to downloader on failure \u2014 but our regexes match. clean_html on title \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso `_search_regex` might use `self._downloader` only in error paths. OK.\n\nNow the fake downloader: needs `.params` dict. _sort_formats: `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')`. Fine.\n\nInfoExtractor.__init__ signature \u2014 let me verify with get_info. Actually I should check. Let me call get_info for InfoExtractor.__init__ and _search_regex maybe. I'll do one more round of get_info for __init__.\n\nAlso need to double check `_match_id` uses cls._VALID_URL \u2014 the real class has it.\n\nAlso `urljoin(url, api_url)` \u2014 api_url like '/api/mp4' joined with 'https://video.udn.com/embed/news/300' \u2192 'https://video.udn.com/api/mp4'. My mock returns the media URL regardless.\n\nLet me also assert result dict: {'id': '300', 'formats': [...], 'title': ..., 'thumbnail': ...}.\n\nFor test 3 formats assertions: m3u8 mock returns format with format_id 'hls' (m3u8_id='hls'), f4m with 'hds'. Assert formats[0]['url'] etc. Also assert _extract_m3u8_formats called with ext='mp4', m3u8_id='hls' \u2014 capture kwargs.\n\nAlso test 1: assert result == {'_type': 'url', 'url': yt_url, 'ie_key': 'Youtube'} and that no further download happened (download_webpage called once, for the page).\n\nStructure: a helper to build the ie and patch. Write tests:\n\n1. test_youtube_redirect\n2. test_fallback_regex_parsing (else branch, mobj + no-mobj + poster None)\n3. test_full_extraction (if branch, m3u8/f4m/http, empty api_url skip)\n\nAlso maybe test options parse failure with poster present? Test 2 covers poster default None; could add poster in test 2 too but then poster regex default isn't exercised... line 59-60 executes either way (the call happens; default only matters if no match). To cover the branch where default is used, omit poster. Fine.\n\nLet me check InfoExtractor.__init__.",
